when daria was five months pregnant she  felt dangerously ill with the bacterial  infection listeria she was filled with  fear told there was a strong chance  she'd lose her baby  but it was after luke was born that she  really struggled i felt very very low  and  apart from a handful of very close  people to me  i kept getting  answers such as you know you should feel  grateful  your son is alive his son is well just  basically needed some reassurance  that  you know i'm actually going to be fine  and that i was not alone  she was given antidepressants and  directed towards an online self-help  program but felt the depth of her  depression was dismissed  and daria isn't alone  the mental health charity pandas which  provide support during the perinatal  period covering pregnancy and the first  year post-childbirth  says thousands of struggling parents are  not getting adequate help from the nhs  when asked about their experiences more  than a quarter of those seeking support  said they felt their concerns were not  taken seriously  two in five said they had to wait too  long to receive help and three quarters  said their condition got worse in that  time  hovid hasn't helped putting immense  pressure on the nhs  it means demand for the charities  services is 70 higher than before the  pandemic lots of parents plan on having  a baby and they have a key  kind of  vision of what that would look like but  due to lockdown restrictions  and the pandemic itself  that journey has been very very  different the government says community  perinatal health services have been  rolled out countrywide with more than 40  000 women seen last year  but daria believes too many are still  being left to cope alone  now she volunteers for the charity that  helped her when she needed it most  emma birchley sky muse south london
 

